.TH FETCHIP 1 2026-09-28 fetchip "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fetchip \- reliable, non-forking public IP lookup
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fetchip
.RB [ \-4 | \-6 ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR type ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.br
.B fetchip
.B \-\-list
.BR type | name
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fetchip
writes the host's public IP address to standard output, or exits with an error.
The address is always valid and in canonical form.
Apart from the output of
.B \-\-help
and
.BR \-\-list ,
nothing else is ever written to standard output.
.PP
It asks several public "what is my IP" services, drawn in random order a few at a time, and prints an address only when they agree.
An address wins once at least two services report it and it holds a strict majority of the answers so far in its family.
IPv4 and IPv6 answers are tallied separately, so without
.B \-4
or
.B \-6
a dual-stack host prints whichever family's address wins first.
When the remaining services can no longer produce a winner,
.B fetchip
exits with an error.
.PP
.B fetchip
speaks HTTP, HTTPS and DNS itself, in one process; it never runs another program.
Host names are looked up through
.BR systemd-resolved (8),
which must be running.
Every query has a timeout, and outstanding queries are cancelled once the outcome is settled.
DNS services are queried at the provider's own server, not through the system resolver.
Where that server is authoritative for the name (akamai-dns, google, akahelp),
a reply without the authoritative-answer flag is refused,
since something on the path answered in its place.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-4
Fetch the public IPv4 address.
.TP
.B \-6
Fetch the public IPv6 address.
.TP
.BI \-s " type" "\fR, \fP\-\-service=" type
Ask only services of one type:
.BR HTTP ,
.BR HTTPS ,
.BR DNS ,
.BR DNS_A ,
.B DNS_AAAA
or
.BR DNS_TXT .
.B DNS
selects all three DNS types.
The type is not case sensitive.
.B HTTP
implies
.BR \-i .
.TP
.BI \-n " name" "\fR, \fP\-\-name=" name
Ask only the named service and print its answer, without consensus.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-insecure
Use the plain HTTP endpoints instead of HTTPS.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Copy log messages to standard error.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.TP
.BR \-\-list " " type | name
Print the values
.B \-s
or
.B \-n
accepts, one per line.
The bash completion uses this.
.SH SERVICES
.TP
.B HTTP and HTTPS
ifconfig.me, icanhazip, ipecho, ident.me, dnsomatic, amazon, akamai, ipinfo, ipify
.TP
.B DNS_A and DNS_AAAA
opendns, quad9
.TP
.B DNS_A
akamai-dns
.TP
.B DNS_TXT
google, akahelp
.SH LOGGING
.B fetchip
logs to the systemd journal; read it with
.BR "journalctl \-t fetchip" .
Debug messages are journaled only by a debug build.
With
.BR \-v ,
every message, debug included, is also written to standard error.
Option errors are always written to standard error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
An address was written to standard output.
.TP
.B 1
Nothing was written to standard output.
.SH EXAMPLES
Use the address in a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ip=$(fetchip \-4) || exit 1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask one DNS service and watch what it does:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fetchip \-v \-n quad9
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR journalctl (1)
